# "build exe" = merged + fresh code only

When the user says **build exe**, never build from the working branch as-is.
An exe built from a branch that is missing merged fixes, or that predates the
current branch head, is an **old build** and is not acceptable.

## Required order (no shortcuts)

1. **Everything pending must be merged first.** Check every open PR from this
   work (`gh pr list --state open`). If any of them is still open, stop and tell
   the user which PRs must be merged before the exe can be built. Do not build
   in the meantime. Never merge PRs yourself.
2. **Fetch fresh master**: `git fetch origin master`. Confirm the local branch
   contains `origin/master` (`git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/master HEAD`)
   and has **no unpushed or uncommitted code**. If the branch is behind master,
   rebase or merge master in first and push.
3. **Confirm the tree is what will ship**: `git status --short` must be empty
   (apart from the build stamp) and `git diff origin/master --stat` must show
   only intended, already-pushed work. Print the HEAD SHA that is being built.
4. Only then run the build (Wine + PyInstaller on Linux, `build_exe.ps1` on
   Windows), stamp with the current minute, stage `downloads/ConfigScanner.exe`,
   update `downloads/README.txt` + `README.md` stamp/SHA256 lines.
5. After the build, verify the PYZ inside the exe actually contains the newest
   modules (grep the decompressed module bytes for a symbol added in the latest
   commit) before pushing.
6. In the summary, state the **HEAD SHA**, **stamp**, **SHA256**, and which
   commits are included. If any fix is *not* in the exe, say so in the first
   sentence.

## Never

- Build while a PR of this work is still open or the branch is behind master.
- Ship a build and describe a later fix as "not yet in the exe" as a footnote.
- Build the same stamp twice; every build gets the current minute.
